# An explicit `using` clause must resolve by TYPE, not by declaration order

Executable record of BUGS.md `ref-front-injects-the-first-given-for-an-explicit-using-clause`,
which had no gate.

Two `given`s for the same trait at different type arguments. `z(List("a", "b"))` needs
`M[String]`; picking the FIRST declared `given` instead of the matching one is the defect, and
it is invisible whenever the first declaration happens to be the right one — so the case calls
`z` at BOTH types, in the order that makes the wrong choice observable.

```scalascript
trait M[A]:
  def empty: A

given intM: M[Int] with
  def empty: Int = 0

given strM: M[String] with
  def empty: String = "x"

def z[A](xs: List[A])(using m: M[A]): A = m.empty

def main(): Unit =
  println(z(List("a", "b")))
  println(z(List(1, 2)))

main()
```
